Calc: an .xlsx keeps the cached results of its filled-down (shared) formulas when it is opened.

The import set the cached number of a shared formula but left the cell dirty, unlike a formula of its own
(applyCellFormulaValues resets it). A dirty cell is calculated again by the first reader of its value, and the
optimal row height pass at the end of the import (ScDocShell::UpdateAllRowHeights, through
ScColumn::GetOptimalHeight -> HasEditCells -> GetRangeScriptType -> ScCellFormat::GetString) reads every cell. So a
large workbook of filled-down SUMIF/VLOOKUP/COUNTIF columns was calculated in full while loading, although formulas
are never recalculated on load by default (OOXMLRecalcMode = 1): a 24 MB synthetic workbook of 200 000 rows with
a SUMIF(B:B;B2;K:K) column took 160 s on 32 cores, and on a 4-core PC many minutes, looking like a hang. The same
workbook with every formula written out per cell (no shared formulas) opened in 24 s.

The shared path now resets the dirty flag as the non-shared path does. A document whose formula results are all 0
or missing is still recalculated in full (WorkbookFragment::recalcFormulaCells), and OOXMLRecalcMode "always" or
"ask" behaves as before.

